<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Council of Venice</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1172">1172</a>–<a href="/years/1797">1797</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Major Council, Maggior Consiglio, Mazor Consegio</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Council was the chief political assembly of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> from 1172 to 1797, electing most magistracies and senior councils, passing laws and exercising judicial oversight. At first relatively open, it was reshaped by the Serrata, or lock-out, of 28 February 1297, which kept existing members and more than doubled the council to over 1,100 men by 1300; from 1323 membership was restricted to men whose ancestors had held high office, making it hereditary. Its members thus became the nobility of Venice, almost 200 families, later recorded in the Golden Book, while a class of citizens stood between them and the common people. On 12 May 1797 it voted to end the Republic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-council-of-venice.jpg" alt="Great Council of Venice" width="330" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francesco Guardi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guardi_-_Le_Doge_remercie_le_Conseil_majeur,_vers_1770_-_1780,_INV_20800.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
